@startuml report_diagram

' =====================================================================
'  report/ — the experiment log, documented on its own.
'
'  Kept OUT of the main class diagram on purpose. It is a debugging aid
'  still being validated, and it is orthogonal to the simulation: the
'  engine, the fleet and the algorithms are unaware it exists. Folding
'  five more classes into the main diagram would have cost the reader
'  more than it told them.
'
'  report attaches through SimulationDriver and through nothing else: it
'  implements the interface so a frontend cannot tell a collector from a
'  controller, and it measures the run from the StepSnapshot that same
'  interface already returns. No concrete class of client.control is
'  named here — see the note on RunCollector.
'
'  Regenerate:  plantuml diagrammes/report_diagram.puml
' =====================================================================

title report — Experiment log (opt-in, behind main.py --log)

skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam classFontSize 13
skinparam ArrowColor #333333
skinparam ClassBorderColor #4A90D9
skinparam ClassBackgroundColor #EAF4FF
skinparam ClassHeaderBackgroundColor #4A90D9
skinparam ClassHeaderFontColor #FFFFFF
skinparam PackageBorderColor #888888
skinparam PackageBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam NoteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7
skinparam NoteBorderColor #C9B458
skinparam linetype ortho

' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
'  External: the one seam report attaches to.
' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
package "client · control" #EEEEEE {
    abstract class SimulationDriver <<Strategy>> #FFFFFF {
        + {abstract} step() : StepSnapshot
        + {abstract} run(steps: int) : list[StepSnapshot]
        + {abstract} snapshot() : StepSnapshot
        + {abstract} send_batch_to(...) : list[Task]
    }
}

package "client · view" #EEEEEE {
    class StepSnapshot <<DTO>> #FFFFFF {
        + step, goals, result
        + tasks : tuple[TaskView]
        + completed, failed
    }
}

' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
'  report
' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
package "report" #F0FFF0 {

    class RunCollector <<Decorator>> {
        - _driver : SimulationDriver
        - _config : ScenarioConfig
        - _label  : str
        - _run_id : str
        --
        .. driving (forwarded) ..
        + step() : StepSnapshot
        + run(steps: int) : list[StepSnapshot]
        + snapshot() : StepSnapshot
        + send_batch_to(...) : list[Task]
        .. measuring ..
        - _observe(snap: StepSnapshot)       : void
        - _observe_tasks(snap: StepSnapshot) : void
        - _observe_agents(snap: StepSnapshot): void
        .. result: three renderings of one observation ..
        + record(label: str = None) : RunRecord
        + events() : list[TaskEvent]
        + trace() : list[AgentTrace]
    }
    note bottom of RunCollector
      **Measures a run without the run knowing**
      It wraps a driver and implements the same interface, so a frontend
      drives it exactly as it drives the controller. Only ``main.py --log``
      builds one; without the flag the frontend gets the controller itself
      and nothing here runs.

      It measures the **frame, not the engine**: everything it records comes
      from the StepSnapshot that ``step()`` already returns. It holds no
      Simulation, no FleetManager, no Grid — so the metrics can be reworked,
      and they will be, without the engine or the fleet changing.

      **Why the interface suffices.** The handles it used to need (``sim``,
      ``fleet``, ``queue``) left SimulationDriver so that a *frontend* could
      no longer reach the engine through it. A collector could have kept them
      by naming the concrete controller — measuring does justify reaching
      further than drawing does. It does not need to: a snapshot carries the
      task states, the goals and every agent's position, which is the whole
      of what is measured here. One seam narrowed and none widened.

      Per-tick observation is not an optimisation: FleetManager prunes a
      task on the very tick it ends, so a transition not caught live is
      gone for good — snapshot or not.

      **run_id is fixed at construction**, not at record(): a TaskEvent or
      AgentTrace row observed mid-run must carry the same id as the
      RunRecord written at the end, so all three can be joined back
      together after the fact.
    end note

    class RunRecord <<value object>> {
        .. identity ..
        + run_id, timestamp, schema_version, git_rev, label
        .. scenario ..
        + width, height, agents, obstacles, algo, allocator, mode
        + task_source, seed, patience, max_attempts
        .. run ..
        + steps, wall_ms, step_ms_mean
        .. tasks ..
        + tasks_created / done / failed / unfinished
        + completion_rate, failed_unreachable, failed_stalled
        + reassignments, attempts_max
        .. latency ..
        + wait_mean / max, service_mean / max, detour_mean / max
        .. drift ..
        + drift_agents, drift_mean, drift_max
        + displaced_events, goalless_frac
        .. occupancy ..
        + busy_frac, idle_max
        --
        + {static} columns() : list[str]
        + as_row() : dict
        + {static} sql_create_table(table: str) : str
    }
    note right of RunRecord
      **One schema, two renderings**
      Every field is a plain scalar, because a CSV header and a SQL table
      are the same record shown twice — anything nested would serialise to
      CSV and then have nowhere to go in a relational column.
      ``sql_create_table()`` sits next to the dataclass so the relational
      shape cannot drift from it, and resolves types through
      ``typing.get_type_hints`` rather than by parsing annotation text:
      guessing from the repr silently mistypes exactly the Optional columns.
      ``schema_version`` rides on every row, so a dataset spanning code
      changes stays interpretable; ``git_rev`` + ``seed`` are what make a
      row reproducible after the fact.
    end note

    interface Record <<Protocol>> {
        + {abstract} columns() : list[str]
        + {abstract} as_row() : dict
    }
    note left of Record
      **Structural, not inherited.** RunRecord, TaskEvent and AgentTrace
      each satisfy this by shape alone — a sink is agnostic to which one
      it is writing.
    end note

    class TaskEvent <<value object>> {
        + run_id, step, task_id, event
        + agent_id, x, y, target_x, target_y
        + attempts, reason
        --
        + {static} columns() : list[str]
        + as_row() : dict
    }
    note right of TaskEvent
      **One task-lifecycle transition, one row.**
      event: created | assigned | released | done | failed
      reason (failed only): stalled_watchdog | unreachable_intake
      Answers "what happened to task 7, and why" — the question an
      aggregate count in RunRecord cannot.
    end note

    class AgentTrace <<value object>> {
        + run_id, step, agent_id
        + x, y, goal_x, goal_y
        + task_id, task_state
        --
        + {static} columns() : list[str]
        + as_row() : dict
    }
    note right of AgentTrace
      **One agent, one step, one row.** Verbose by design: meant to be
      filtered or diffed, not read top to bottom. Reach for it only when
      a TaskEvent log does not explain a deadlock or drift by itself.
    end note

    abstract class ResultSink <<Strategy>> {
        + {abstract} append(record: Record) : void
        + append_all(records: Iterable[Record]) : void
    }

    class CsvSink {
        + path : Path
        --
        + append(record: Record) : void
        + append_all(records: Iterable[Record]) : void
        - _check_header(columns: list[str]) : void
    }
    note bottom of CsvSink
      **Appending to a changed schema raises.**
      A new column would otherwise shift every later value one place left,
      producing a file that still parses and is quietly wrong.
      Swapping in SQL later is one new ResultSink subclass and nothing
      else — verified by loading the CSV into sqlite with
      ``sql_create_table()`` unedited.

      ``append_all`` opens the file once for every row instead of once
      per row — the difference between one header check and thousands,
      which matters once a trace is the thing being written.
    end note

    class NullSink {
        + append(record: Record) : void
    }
}

' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
'  Relations
' ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
SimulationDriver <|.. RunCollector
RunCollector "1" o-- "1" SimulationDriver : "_driver (decorates)"
RunCollector ..> StepSnapshot : «measures»
RunCollector ..> RunRecord : «produces»
RunCollector ..> TaskEvent : «produces»
RunCollector ..> AgentTrace : «produces»

RunRecord ..|> Record
TaskEvent ..|> Record
AgentTrace ..|> Record

ResultSink <|.. CsvSink
ResultSink <|.. NullSink
ResultSink ..> Record : «reads»

note as N1
  **How main.py wires it**
    driver = build(config)
    if --log:  driver = RunCollector(driver, config)
    Frontend(driver).run(steps)
    finally:
      CsvSink(--log).append(driver.record())
      CsvSink(FILE.events.csv).append_all(driver.events())
      CsvSink(FILE.trace.csv).append_all(driver.trace())

  Written in a ``finally``, so a window closed by hand or a Ctrl-C still
  yields an observation — losing those is how a session's worth of
  experiments quietly goes missing. A logging failure is reported without
  masking the reason the run ended: if the run itself raised, that
  traceback is the useful one. ``append_all`` on an empty list writes
  nothing — a static-mode run gets no ``.events.csv``, since it has no
  tasks to report on.
end note
N1 .. RunCollector

